2et8|v|of Vibrate ; To brandish; to move to and fro; to swing; as, to vibrate a sword or a staff. ; To mark or measure by moving to and fro; as, a pendulum vibrating seconds. ; To affect with vibratory motion; to set in vibration. ; To move to and fro, or from side to side, as a pendulum, an elastic rod, or a stretched string, when disturbed from its position of rest; to swing; to oscillate. ; To have the constituent particles move to and fro, with alternate compression and dilation of parts, as the air, or any elastic body; to quiver. ; To produce an oscillating or quivering effect of sound; as, a whisper vibrates on the ear. ; To pass from one state to another; to waver; to fluctuate; as, a man vibrates between two opinions.	o:1el8,33,5~1el8,33,5	s:1d9h,49,6~1d9h,49,6	d:24i6,18,9~24i6,18,9
